WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.003
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.003 --> 00:00:04.170
JONATHAN GRUBER: I think
the main thing an economics

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.875
course can do in
this day and age

00:00:05.875 --> 00:00:08.250
is get people to be a little
more flexible, a little more

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.792
thoughtful about
decisions they make,

00:00:09.792 --> 00:00:11.892
and seeing both
sides of the problem.

00:00:11.892 --> 00:00:13.350
SARAH HANSEN: Today
in the podcast:

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.667
seeing economics in
a whole new light.

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:17.250
JONATHAN GRUBER:
I've had application,

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.050
a course about
demand and supply,

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.880
about Kim Kardashian
tweeting herself

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.980
out a picture of
an exercise corset,

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.385
and that increased demand
for exercise corsets.

00:00:25.385 --> 00:00:27.510
And so thinking about how
something, where students

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:28.927
can see it and
say, "Wow, I hadn't

00:00:28.927 --> 00:00:32.748
realized that economics help
me think about that problem."

00:00:32.748 --> 00:00:34.290
SARAH HANSEN: Welcome
to Chalk Radio,

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.020
a podcast about inspired
teaching at MIT.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.850
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen,
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:45.330
In this episode, we'll be
talking about the course 14.01

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.560
Introductory Microeconomics.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.340
This course is filled
with unique and creative

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.180
applications for microeconomics
in our everyday lives.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.700
I'll be chatting today with
the creator of this course,

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.460
Professor Jon Gruber.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.280
One of the things I was
most excited to find out

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.099
was the way people
perceive economics.

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:08.430
So I asked Professor Gruber
what misconceptions students

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.103
tend to bring to this class.

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:11.770
JONATHAN GRUBER:
That's a good question.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:16.920
I think they tend to perceive
it as maybe softer than it is,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.720
maybe not perceive
that you're going

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.600
to derive fairly
sharp tools that

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.250
are going to help you explain a
lot of the world fairly easily.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:24.708
Then, on the other
hand, they often

00:01:24.708 --> 00:01:27.300
come with expectations that
we'll explain more of the world

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.120
than we can.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.662
SARAH HANSEN: So
how do you help them

00:01:29.662 --> 00:01:31.155
address these misconceptions?

00:01:31.155 --> 00:01:33.030
JONATHAN GRUBER: I think
by just being honest

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:36.450
about the limitations and
benefits of our models,

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.910
by explaining the power
that our models can

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:40.770
deliver in fairly simple
terms in explaining

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:41.970
a lot of the world.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.880
But at the same
time, representing

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.630
the things the models
can't easily explain

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.250
and being honest
about that and trying

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.458
to get students interested
in studying more economics

00:01:49.458 --> 00:01:51.180
that can explain
those things as well.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.430
I don't expect most
viewers of my videos

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.872
to go on to get a PhD in
economics, unfortunately.

00:01:55.872 --> 00:01:58.080
I expect they're going to
go out and have other jobs,

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.663
do other things, and I want them
to be more educated consumers

00:02:00.663 --> 00:02:04.080
of news and to understand what's
going on in the world in terms

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.695
of economic policy.

00:02:05.695 --> 00:02:08.070
SARAH HANSEN: Could we talk
a minute about the real world

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:08.565
applications?

00:02:08.565 --> 00:02:09.060
JONATHAN GRUBER: OK.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.518
SARAH HANSEN: It's a really
interesting part of the course.

00:02:11.518 --> 00:02:14.970
You have LeBron James's college
decision, the Uber surge

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:15.717
pricing.

00:02:15.717 --> 00:02:17.550
Tell us about your
decision to really anchor

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.402
the course with these
real world applications.

00:02:20.402 --> 00:02:22.360
JONATHAN GRUBER: It's
what makes economics fun.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.450
Once again, I'm
not training people

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:25.770
to be economics PhDs here.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.262
I'm training people to
think like an economist,

00:02:28.262 --> 00:02:29.970
and thinking like an
economist, if you've

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.250
got a very facile
mind and you look

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.930
at the basic theory
videos I teach you,

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.173
you can understand it.

00:02:35.173 --> 00:02:36.840
But you only really
understand something

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.632
when you go in the real
world and apply it.

00:02:38.632 --> 00:02:42.390
And so it's a chance for people
to see the ways that economics

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.390
rules the world but
to also sort of apply

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.880
what they've learned to make
sure they understand it.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.422
SARAH HANSEN: What
tips for educators

00:02:49.422 --> 00:02:52.283
do you have about selecting
really good applications?

00:02:52.283 --> 00:02:53.700
JONATHAN GRUBER:
I think it's just

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.000
a matter of trying to find
examples in the real world that

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.860
are relatable.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.250
Companies they've heard of
and issues they think about

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.560
and that-- in a not
necessarily obvious way--

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.940
teach you about the
lessons you want to learn.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:09.810
I've had application, the
course about demand and supply,

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.610
about Kim Kardashian
tweeting herself

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.710
out a picture of
an exercise corset,

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:16.187
and that increased demand
for exercise corsets.

00:03:16.187 --> 00:03:18.270
And so thinking about how
something where students

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.645
can see it and
say, wow, I hadn't

00:03:19.645 --> 00:03:21.930
realized economics helped
me think about that problem.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.650
SARAH HANSEN: Professor Gruber's
passion for economics is clear,

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:29.740
and I wanted to get
to the bottom of it.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.230
Why does he care so much
about helping others

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:34.000
find their passion
for economics,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.150
and how did his own
curiosity begin?

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.340
JONATHAN GRUBER: I
came to MIT knowing

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.100
that I was good at math,
but I didn't really like it.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.680
What I liked was real world
social policy types of issues,

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.370
and I took 14.01
and said, oh my god,

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.170
I can use math to apply
to the real world.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:51.010
Isn't this cool?

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:52.835
And I've been hooked ever since.

00:03:52.835 --> 00:03:54.460
Many of your listeners
will not realize

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:55.420
that there was a
time when we used

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.130
to talk to people
next to us on a plane,

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:58.552
before we had smartphones.

00:03:58.552 --> 00:04:01.135
And every time I fly in a plane
and tell the person next to me

00:04:01.135 --> 00:04:03.040
I was an economics
professor, they'd say, oh,

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.350
that was the worst course
I ever took, and I thought,

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:06.670
that can't be true.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:07.600
It might not be the
best, but there's

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.850
no way it could be the worst
course someone ever took.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.760
I realized it just was generally
badly taught in our school

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.410
systems, and so I
was always interested

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:15.493
in trying to address that.

00:04:15.493 --> 00:04:16.959
But I think really,
where I really

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:20.769
want to go is figure out a
way to write better problems.

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:23.228
It's easy to write a problem
with a mathematical solution:

00:04:23.228 --> 00:04:25.270
"The firm has the following
production functions.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:27.430
Solve for their
profit maximization."

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:28.900
It's harder to
write problems that

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.970
involve math but also
derive intuition,

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.470
and that's really
where I want to go

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.730
is, help students bridge
that link between solving

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.730
equations and understanding
why they're doing it

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:38.645
and what they're
learning from it.

00:04:38.645 --> 00:04:41.020
SARAH HANSEN: I just want to
talk about that a little bit

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.450
more, because you're not
the first MIT faculty

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:46.870
member to really want
to hone in on intuition.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:48.340
And I'm just
wondering, like how do

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:50.440
we develop students'
intuition, because I'm

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.260
sure educators out there
in all sorts of fields

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:54.580
are wanting to do this too.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:57.940
Is it something that's developed
over time in a field and just

00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:59.830
with experience,
or is it something

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:02.605
we can cultivate as teachers?

00:05:02.605 --> 00:05:05.230
JONATHAN GRUBER: I think that it
is something we can cultivate.

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.647
I think that it comes from not
assuming people know things

00:05:07.647 --> 00:05:08.660
they don't know.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:10.420
It comes from
teaching in layers.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.520
So that if there's
a set of things

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.980
I teach that I'd be
really disappointed

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:17.930
if not everyone came out
with intuition from my class,

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:19.660
there's other more
subtle concepts

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.078
that if not everyone remembers
them, understands them,

00:05:22.078 --> 00:05:23.620
then that's not the
end of the world.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:25.870
But I'd like students who want
to go on to be like, oh, that's

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:26.370
interesting.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:27.860
I want to go down that path.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:30.760
So I think it's really about
getting the things that you

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.810
really want people to
know, hammering them,

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:35.320
but the other thing I like
that I do in my class is really

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:36.983
always have a constant roadmap.

00:05:36.983 --> 00:05:39.400
I always tell the students,
OK, here's where we came from.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.442
Here's where we're going.

00:05:40.442 --> 00:05:41.500
Here's where it fits in.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.030
So they can have
a holistic sense

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:44.197
of how it all fits together.

00:05:48.473 --> 00:05:49.890
SARAH HANSEN: In
our conversation,

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:52.020
I asked Professor
Gruber what he believes

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.320
educators can do practically
to transfer problems

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:58.530
from the classroom into the real
world, and his answer touched

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:00.210
on a point that is
coming into play

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:02.160
all over our modern world--

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.860
the complexity and truth
about, well, truth.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.410
JONATHAN GRUBER: I think the
main number one thing is just

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:12.260
to teach a healthy respect
for the scientific method,

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:14.810
to teach students that
there are objective truths

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.450
and that there is this long
standing scientific method

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:18.990
for how we get at them.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.380
The other thing,
however, is to recognize

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:24.470
that there are two sides
of almost every issue.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:27.038
And that this is something
that a lot of students at MIT

00:06:27.038 --> 00:06:28.580
sometimes have
problems with my class

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:30.982
which is it's not just an
equation with one right answer.

00:06:30.982 --> 00:06:32.940
There's pros and cons to
a lot of these things.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:36.470
So I think it's teaching a
search for the objective truth

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:39.080
that can help you come to the
conclusion that works for you.

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:41.510
That there's not necessarily
one right answer.

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:43.430
You and I might
disagree about a topic,

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:45.740
but we should agree on
an objective set of facts

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:48.910
and have a scientific method
for getting to our conclusion.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:51.160
I think that's the key-- in
today's world especially--

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:53.270
that's the key is
just getting people

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:56.360
to have a healthy
disagreement rather

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:57.714
than an unhealthy disagreement.

00:07:00.258 --> 00:07:02.800
SARAH HANSEN: One of the most
compelling parts of this course

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.010
is how it emphasizes ways to
carry its concepts forward

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:07.540
into the real world.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.460
Students are given
specific pathways

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.950
they might follow in
their career or lives

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:15.250
outside of school, where
they can use these concepts.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:18.220
This was something really
important to Professor Gruber,

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.920
and I wanted to know why.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:22.900
JONATHAN GRUBER: I
feel that economics

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:23.980
is enormously powerful.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:25.870
I'm what you might call an
imperialistic economist.

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:27.190
I feel like
economics can explain

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:29.690
lots of things in the world,
and there's very few situations

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:32.762
where a good economic framework
can't help you at least go

00:07:32.762 --> 00:07:33.970
forward in thinking about it.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:37.720
And I don't want students
to just take the AP test

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:39.470
and forget about
it, quite frankly.

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:41.470
I want this to be something
they bring with them

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:42.790
and actually keep with them.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:43.990
There's a famous old
Saturday Night Live

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:46.060
skit about what you remember
five years after college,

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:47.815
and it's five minutes, and
three and a half minutes of it

00:07:47.815 --> 00:07:48.790
is spring break.

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.430
I want them to try to have
a basic set of intuitions

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:54.700
that they can understand can
apply to decisions they're

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:58.120
going to make about how
much to save in their 401(k)

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:01.450
to how hard to work,
to what car to choose,

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:03.310
to how to buy a
house, et cetera.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:05.260
And quite frankly,
to me personally,

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:08.110
how to vote and think about
public policy, a set of tools

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:10.270
that they can bring
to bear on thinking

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:12.897
about these important
problems in America today.

00:08:12.897 --> 00:08:14.980
One slight difficulty we
have is a lot of students

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:17.123
come in much too determined
and set in their ways

00:08:17.123 --> 00:08:18.290
in what they're going to do.

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:19.110
I'm going to be an engineer.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:20.530
I'm going to be a
computer scientist,

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:22.113
and they're often
not flexible enough.

00:08:22.113 --> 00:08:23.958
So part of I view my
job, and a lot of times

00:08:23.958 --> 00:08:25.750
when they're taking
economics, they're just

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:27.250
taking it because they have to.

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:30.622
And my job is to get them to
say, hey, I actually like this,

00:08:30.622 --> 00:08:33.039
and I love nothing more when
I get an email from a student

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:34.720
saying, wow, I wasn't even
going to study economics.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:37.012
But I like your course, and
want to major in economics.

00:08:37.012 --> 00:08:39.095
That was wonderful, but
I also want the student

00:08:39.095 --> 00:08:40.720
that is not going to
major in economics

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.000
to several years later
saying, hey, yeah,

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:45.100
that's something I can
relate what I learned

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:47.270
in economics to how I
think about that problem,

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:50.140
and that could
help me in my life.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.000
Microeconomics is
basic economics.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.220
Macroeconomics is
essentially applied micro.

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:57.850
It's basically
about every decision

00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:59.500
we make, every
decision firms make,

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.660
can all be informed
by economic framework,

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:04.858
and that can be informed in a
particularly healthy way today.

00:09:04.858 --> 00:09:06.400
Because the economic
framework really

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.710
is about trade-offs
and about thinking

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:11.260
the fact that nothing's
easy, everything

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:13.450
involves pros and
cons and trade-offs,

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:16.340
and there's shades of gray
to every decision we make.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:18.940
And I think the main thing
an economics course can

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:21.550
do in this day and age is get
people to be a little more

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:23.380
flexible, a little
more thoughtful

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:24.895
about decisions
they make and seeing

00:09:24.895 --> 00:09:25.978
both sides of the problem.

00:09:30.355 --> 00:09:32.480
SARAH HANSEN: If you're
interested in learning more

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:35.750
about microeconomics or viewing
his real world application

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:39.920
videos, you can visit Professor
Gruber's interactive online MIT

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:41.600
course on edX.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.370
To access his full
lecture videos

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:45.440
from his on-campus
teaching, head over

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.970
to his scholar course on
our site at ocw.mit.edu.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:52.790
You'll also find 20 plus
additional courses just

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:55.010
on microeconomics on OCW.

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:58.910
If you're an instructor, head on
over to the OCW Educator Portal

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:05.610
at ocw.mit.edu/educator to
find resources just for you.

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:06.650
Thanks for listening.

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:11.090
